**Day 39: SQL vs NoSQL – When to Use**  

**Topic:**  
Comparison of SQL (relational) and NoSQL (non-relational) databases, focusing on structured vs. unstructured data, ACID vs. BASE principles, scalability, and real-world applications.  

**Learning Objectives:**  
• Understand key differences between SQL and NoSQL databases.  
• Evaluate use cases for structured vs. unstructured data.  
• Analyze trade-offs between ACID (consistency) and BASE (availability) models.  

**Activities / Deliverables:**  
• Research and document SQL vs. NoSQL strengths/weaknesses.  
• Case study: Justify database choice for an e-commerce product catalog.  
• Prepare a summary report in MS Word.  

**Important Concepts:**  
• **Structured data:** Fixed schema (e.g., tables in SQL).  
• **Unstructured data:** Flexible schema (e.g., JSON in NoSQL).  
• **Scalability:** SQL (vertical) vs. NoSQL (horizontal).  

**SQL Syntax / Commands:**  
• `CREATE TABLE`, `JOIN`, `SELECT` (SQL).  
• `db.collection.insert()` (NoSQL, e.g., MongoDB).  

**Real-world Use Cases:**  
• **SQL:** Banking systems (ACID compliance).  
• **NoSQL:** Social media feeds (scalability for unstructured data).  

**Tools / Tech:**  
• SQL: MySQL, PostgreSQL.  
• NoSQL: MongoDB, Cassandra.  

**Summary:**  
SQL excels in structured, transactional data, while NoSQL offers flexibility and scalability for unstructured or rapidly evolving datasets. The choice depends on use case requirements.  

